id: preamble
title: Read this first
numbered: no
---
[% WRAPPER hero eyebrow='A note from the author' h1='How to Produce Production-Quality Code with AI' %]

This training is based on [PAAD](https://github.com/ovid/paad), a open-source
set of skills for AI coding assistants, built on one idea: producing
production-quality code. This training is free, complete, and I'm not selling
anything. It's based on a year-and-a-half of hands-on work, experimentation,
and many development teams trying it, not guesses. PAAD uses
**engineering-driven AI**, not AI-driven engineering, the meaning of which
becomes clear as the course goes on. You're in control.

I hate the term "AI slop." AI slop is just a rebranding of "technical debt."
You know how to manage technical debt, so you know how to manage AI slop, but
slapping the word "AI" on it scares people into thinking it's something
strange. It is not.

If you let the AI do the driving, you're going to fail, just like all those
horror stories you see on the internet about companies failing because of
vibe-coded slop. PAAD lets *you* do the driving, but still leverage the
strengths of AI. You can write great code with AI, but only if you stay in the
driver's seat.

Unlike other skill frameworks that I have tried, PAAD isn't trying to
determine your workflow, or enforce a particular way of working. It simply
mirrors what you're probably already doing, day-to-day:

* Take a ticket, work on it
* Make sure the result is good
* Fix tech debt when you can

That's it. It works _with_ the skills you already have and doesn't try to
replace them. It simply makes them better.

[% END %]

As of this writing (November, 2026), I know of companies using PAAD in:

* The United States
* Switzerland
* The UK
* Brazil
* France
* Malta
* Georgia
* Germany
* North Macedonia
* Serbia

And from what I can tell from my GitHub and Google Analytics, there are many
more companies using PAAD that I don't know about. It's currently being cloned
almost 100 times a day.

That being said, I teach software development teams how to write
"production-quality code" with AI. By "production-quality," I simply mean that
the code does what it's supposed to do and most developers can take the code
and say, "yeah, I can work with this." However, I've found that for those
teams who _really_ lean into PAAD, they're often writing _better_ code than
other teams, and even those who hand-code everything. It's not that
hand-coding is bad, but we have deadlines, poor requirements, legacy code to
work around, and so on. PAAD makes it easy to fix this.

The beginning training I give is most of what you need and it only takes a
day. However, I have to follow up with the teams to reinforce practices, learn
from them what I need to change, and deal with their unique challenges. That's
hard to do and this document cannot do it. However, it will give you most of
what you need and if you have an inquisitive mind, you can figure out the
rest.

There are other problems with using this document instead of team training.
Some of this is the fact that there's a hell of a lot I've had to leave out of
here, such as theory. But the core is solid. However, learning a new
methodology means the team needs to understand _as a group_.  I've found that
when they learn separately, their inexperience means they'll have different
interpretations of what's presented here. That's a problem you'll have to
figure out. It also means that if you're a solo developer, there's a good
chance that you might misunderstand some bits. However, if you understand and
follow the loop diagrams [in the PAAD README
document](htts://github.com/ovid/paad), you'll be fine.

Also, this document can't hear your concerns, so I can't answer them. So if a
module confused you, if a skill didn't behave as you expected, or you get
stuck between reading this and running this, my apologies. There's only so
much a document can do.

But read it. More than once. You'll learn a lot.

<p style="color:var(--text-faint);font-size:14px">— Curtis &quot;Ovid&quot; Poe</p>
